## The mask

`boneMask(bones, { root, falloff })` says which bones the layer drives, and how
much. It is pure: a bone hierarchy is names and parents (`boneHierarchy(skeleton)`
reads one), so it needs no skeleton, renderer or GLB. `findBone(bones,
UPPER_BODY_ROOTS)` finds the split root across rig naming styles.

The masking is easy; making it not look terrible is the work:

- **A hard boundary is a mannequin sawn in half.** If one spine bone takes the aim
  and the next the walk, the torso kinks at one joint. Real rigs spread a twist
  over the whole chain, so the mask is a WEIGHT PER BONE, ramped along the chain.
  `falloff` is how many bones it takes to reach full authority.
- **Counter-rotation is what makes legs look attached.** A walk swings the torso
  against the hips. Override the upper body wholesale and the legs swing under a
  dead trunk. That is why `MaskMode` defaults to **additive**: an aim applied as a
  delta from its own reference pose keeps what the locomotion was doing. Override
  is there for when you really want to replace (a reload that must look the same
  whatever the legs do).
- **A mask whose root cannot be found says so.** Rigs disagree about bone names,
  and a missing root gives an all-zero mask, which animates nothing and looks like
  a clip that failed to load.

## How it is done, and the one thing Babylon does not give you

A Babylon `AnimationGroup` is a list of `TargetedAnimation` — an animation and
the node it drives — and its weight applies to the WHOLE group. There is no
per-bone weight.

So a graded mask becomes **one group per weight tier**. With `falloff: 2` the
mask has weights 1/3, 2/3 and 1, which is three groups played at those weights,
plus the base clip playing at the complement. Public API throughout, no
patching, and the tier count is small because the ramp is short.

⚠️ **Babylon has no native ADDITIVE layer.** Its animation weights BLEND — a
weighted average of poses — where an additive layer would apply a delta on top
of whatever is underneath. The mask offers `MaskMode.additive` because it
is the right model and the mask maths is the same either way; what this module
implements is the blended form, which is what the engine supports.

The practical difference is smaller than it sounds, and worth knowing rather
than discovering: with a feathered boundary the lower spine is a genuine blend
of walk and aim, so *some* of the walk's counter-rotation survives into the
torso. What you do not get is the full counter-rotation under a fully-authored
aim pose. If that turns out to matter, true additive means computing per-bone
deltas each frame — real work, and worth measuring the blended version first.

## Names are the seam, so they are checked

Targets are `TransformNode`s named after their bone (`mixamorig:Spine2` on the
stock rig), and a mask keyed on names nobody has is a mask that drives nothing —
silently, and identically to a clip that failed to load. `layerGroups` reports
what it matched so a caller can refuse rather than animate air.

# sphere-map

**Where a place on the ground is on the globe, and back.** A terrain in
`surfaceType="sphere"` is a flat sheet wrapped round a planet: walk far enough
east and you come back. This is the arithmetic of that wrapping, as pure
functions, so that a planet seen from orbit and the ground you land on can
agree about where things are.

## Demo

A small world, and the same world as a globe hung in its own sky. The globe
is not a picture of the terrain: it is built from the terrain's own height
function (`terrain.planetSurface()`), so its coasts are the coasts below. The
red pin is where the camera is, and the globe turns to keep your side of the
world facing you. Fly about (arrow keys, drag to look) and the land under the
pin changes; the readout is your latitude and longitude.

```js
import { b3d, b3dTerrain, b3dPlanet, b3dWater, b3dSun, b3dSkybox, b3dLight, latLonToDirection } from 'tosijs-3d'
import { elements } from 'tosijs'

const { div } = elements
const readout = div({ class: 'readout' }, 'finding the ground…')

// A whole planet 4 km in radius, so that a globe of it shows real relief.
const terrain = b3dTerrain({
  id: 'sphere-map-ground',
  seed: 11, biome: 'on', surfaceType: 'sphere', radius: 4000,
  tileSize: 128, lodLevels: 3, splitFactor: 2, reach: 4000,
  grossScale: 0.0016, detailScale: 0.012, grossAmplitude: 320, detailAmplitude: 24,
  center: true,
})
// biome: 'on' so the globe shades with the ground's own climate and colours;
// flatSea because this world has a sea at 0 m (the water below).
// `terrain` makes it a globe OF that ground: drawn from it, and again
// whenever the ground's shape changes.
const globe = b3dPlanet({ terrain: '#sphere-map-ground', radius: 40, relief: 2, flatSea: 'on', atmosphere: 0.05, subdivisions: 96, biome: 'on' })

preview.append(
  b3d(
    {
      sceneCreated(el, BABYLON) {
        const scene = el.scene
        const cam = new BABYLON.FreeCamera('fly', new BABYLON.Vector3(0, 420, -260), scene)
        cam.setTarget(new BABYLON.Vector3(0, 380, 200))
        cam.speed = 12
        cam.maxZ = 12000
        cam.attachControl(el.querySelector('canvas'), true)
        el.setActiveCamera(cam)

        const pin = BABYLON.MeshBuilder.CreateSphere('pin', { diameter: 3 }, scene)
        const red = new BABYLON.StandardMaterial('pin', scene)
        red.emissiveColor = new BABYLON.Color3(1, 0.15, 0.1)
        red.disableLighting = true
        pin.material = red

        scene.onBeforeRenderObservable.add(() => {
          const root = globe.rootNode
          if (root == null || typeof terrain.latLonAt !== 'function') return
          // The globe rides ahead of the camera, like a map on a stand.
          root.position.set(cam.position.x + 60, cam.position.y + 10, cam.position.z + 190)
          // The camera's place in the world: scene position plus the
          // terrain's floating origin.
          const off = terrain.originOffset
          const here = terrain.latLonAt(cam.position.x + off.x, cam.position.z + off.z)
          if (here == null) return
          const d = latLonToDirection(here.lat, here.lon)
          // The pin sits ON the globe, and the globe turns to show you the
          // side you are on (yaw only, so north stays up).
          pin.parent = root
          pin.position.set(d.x * 43, d.y * 43, d.z * 43)
          const toCam = cam.position.subtract(root.position)
          root.rotation.y = Math.atan2(toCam.x, toCam.z) - Math.atan2(d.x, d.z)
          readout.textContent = `${Math.abs(here.lat).toFixed(2)}° ${here.lat >= 0 ? 'N' : 'S'}   ${Math.abs(here.lon).toFixed(2)}° ${here.lon >= 0 ? 'E' : 'W'}`
        })
      },
    },
    b3dSun({}),
    b3dSkybox({ timeOfDay: 10, realtimeScale: 0 }),
    b3dLight({ intensity: 0.5 }),
    terrain,
    b3dWater({ y: 0, waterSize: 9000, follow: true }),
    globe,
  ),
  readout,
)
```
```css
.preview { height: 100%; position: relative; }
.readout { position: absolute; left: 12px; bottom: 12px; padding: 6px 10px; border-radius: 6px; background: rgba(0,0,0,0.6); color: #fff; font: 13px ui-monospace, monospace; }
```

## Three names for one place

Three ways of naming one place:

| | |
| --- | --- |
| **direction** | a unit vector from the planet's centre. What a globe is made of |
| **latitude, longitude** | degrees. What a person writes down. North is +Y |
| **ground** | metres east (+x) and north (+z) of the terrain's origin, in the terrain's own unshifting coordinates: a scene position plus `terrain.originOffset`. What the terrain uses |

`frame` says how the sheet is laid on the sphere: the planet's `radius` and
the `worldU`, `worldV` the terrain's origin sits at.

```javascript
import { groundToLatLon, latLonToGround, latLonToDirection } from 'tosijs-3d/sphere-map'

const frame = { radius: 6000, worldU: 0, worldV: 0.25 }
const pin = { lat: 12, lon: 40 }
latLonToGround(pin.lat, pin.lon, frame) // where to stand: { x, z } in metres
latLonToDirection(pin.lat, pin.lon)     // where to put the marker on the globe
```

The wrapping is the plain latitude and longitude one, so a metre east is the
same number of degrees at every latitude and the ground is squeezed
east-to-west toward the poles. It is exact at the equator and good across the
middle latitudes; a pole is a line, not a point.

# retarget

**One humanoid's animation on another humanoid's body.** The Universal
Animation Library's clips are authored on the UE mannequin; a Mixamo
character, a Kenney chibi or a model of your own has different bone names,
a different number of bones, a different rest pose and different
proportions. This makes the clips play anyway.

## Demo

One clip, four bodies. On the right, UAL's own mannequin plays the original;
then the same clip retargeted onto a Quaternius superhero (the same 65 bones,
other proportions), omnidude (Mixamo's bones, 0.88 m tall) and a Kenney
chibi (seven bones: one for each whole leg, arm and the torso; shown three
times its size). Pick a clip in the panel.

A chibi's arms are long for its height: hanging where the clip's hang, its
hands would go through the floor. `armSpaceDeg` (Mixamo's "Character
Arm-Space") turns the upper arms out from the body, and the chibi takes 55°.

```js
import { b3d, b3dLight, assetUrl, readSkeleton, retargetClip, select3d } from 'tosijs-3d'
import { tosi } from 'tosijs'

const { rt } = tosi({ rt: { clip: 'Walk_Loop' } })
const clips = ['Idle_Loop', 'Walk_Loop', 'Jog_Fwd_Loop', 'Sprint_Loop', 'Dance_Loop', 'Crouch_Fwd_Loop', 'Swim_Fwd_Loop', 'Pistol_Idle_Loop']

preview.append(
  b3d(
    {
      clearColor: '#2a3340',
      panelTitle: 'Retarget',
      scenePanel: () => [select3d({ label: 'clip', value: rt.clip, options: clips })],
      async sceneCreated(el, BABYLON) {
        const scene = el.scene
        const cam = new BABYLON.ArcRotateCamera('c', Math.PI / 2, 1.35, 7, new BABYLON.Vector3(0, 0.8, 0), scene)
        cam.attachControl(el.querySelector('canvas'), true)
        el.setActiveCamera(cam)
        const ground = BABYLON.MeshBuilder.CreateGround('g', { width: 12, height: 6 }, scene)
        ground.position.y = -0.01

        const load = async (url) => {
          const c = await BABYLON.LoadAssetContainerAsync(url, scene)
          c.addAllToScene()
          for (const g of c.animationGroups) g.stop()
          return c
        }
        const rootOf = (c) => c.meshes.find((m) => m.name === '__root__')
        // One character out of a library file: hide the rest, find its skin.
        const pick = (c, name) => {
          const top = c.transformNodes.find((n) => n.name === name) || c.meshes.find((n) => n.name === name)
          for (const m of c.meshes) if (m.name !== '__root__' && top && !m.isDescendantOf(top) && m !== top) m.setEnabled(false)
          if (top) top.position.set(0, 0, 0)
          return c.skeletons.find((s) => s.bones.some((b) => { const t = b.getTransformNode(); return t && top && t.isDescendantOf(top) }))
        }

        const ual = await load(assetUrl('quaternius/UAL1_core.glb', 2))
        const source = readSkeleton(ual.skeletons[0], rootOf(ual))
        const bodies = [{ root: rootOf(ual), skeleton: null, x: 2.4 }]
        const hero = await load(assetUrl('quaternius/libraries/characters-superhero-male.glb'))
        bodies.push({ root: rootOf(hero), skeleton: pick(hero, 'Superhero_Male_FullBody'), x: 0.8 })
        const omni = await load('/omnidude.glb')
        bodies.push({ root: rootOf(omni), skeleton: omni.skeletons[0], x: -0.8 })
        const mini = await load(assetUrl('kenney/libraries/mini-characters.glb'))
        const miniSkin = pick(mini, 'character-female-a')
        rootOf(mini).scaling.scaleInPlace(3)
        bodies.push({ root: rootOf(mini), skeleton: miniSkin, x: -2.4, armSpaceDeg: 55 })

        for (const b of bodies) {
          b.root.position.x = b.x
          if (b.skeleton) b.read = readSkeleton(b.skeleton, b.root)
        }
        // Retarget each clip once per body, the first time it is asked for.
        const made = new Map()
        let playing = []
        const play = (name) => {
          for (const g of playing) g.stop()
          const clip = ual.animationGroups.find((g) => g.name === name)
          if (!clip) return
          playing = bodies.map((b, i) => {
            if (!b.read) return clip
            const key = name + i
            if (!made.has(key)) made.set(key, retargetClip(clip, source, b.read, { scene, armSpaceDeg: b.armSpaceDeg }))
            return made.get(key)
          })
          for (const g of playing) g.start(true)
        }
        play(rt.clip.value)
        rt.clip.observe(() => play(rt.clip.value))
      },
    },
    b3dLight({ intensity: 0.9 })
  )
)
```

## How it works

1. **Roles.** `humanoidRoles(bones)` says which bone is the hips, the chest,
   the left forearm, … from the names and the hierarchy, across the common
   spellings (UE `upperarm_l`, Mixamo `mixamorig:LeftArm`, Kenney `arm-left`,
   Blender `forearm.L`), and each finger's three joints (`index_01_l`,
   `LeftHandIndex1`, `thumb.01.L`). Twist, IK and end bones are left alone.
2. **Rotations, not positions.** `retargeter(source, target).pose` gives each target bone the
   world-space rotation its source bone made since ITS rest, so bone
   lengths are the target's own: a long-legged clip on a short-legged body
   bends the same joints by the same angles.
3. **Rest poses that disagree.** A clip authored on an A-pose rig, played on
   a T-pose one, must still put the arms where the clip shows them, not 45°
   higher. Each target bone is first lined up with its source bone's rest
   direction (toward the next joint down the limb; a hand toward a finger
   both rigs have), so poses come across as the clip shows them, whatever
   rest pose either rig was bound in.
4. **The hips move in proportion.** Their translation is scaled by the ratio
   of hip heights, so a short character's stride is shorter, not stretched.

Babylon-free and deterministic (plain `{x, y, z, w}` quaternions), like
`fly-by-wire`: the Babylon side (`retarget-babylon`) reads the skeletons and
writes the result.

A mesh with no skeleton at all can be rigged for these clips too: see
[auto-rig](/rig-build/), which fits a skeleton from eight markers dragged
over the mesh, as Mixamo's auto-rigger does, and binds the mesh to it.

# touch-gamepad

Touch/pointer-driven virtual gamepad backed by an SVG. Elements are identified
by `data-part` attributes (not IDs, so multiple instances work).

## Demo

```js
import { gamepadSvg, TouchGamepadSource } from 'tosijs-3d'
import { elements } from 'tosijs'
const { div, pre } = elements

const pad = gamepadSvg()
const customBtns = new Set()
const source = new TouchGamepadSource(pad, {
  handleButton(part, pressed) {
    if (pressed) customBtns.add(part)
    else customBtns.delete(part)
  },
})
const readout = pre({ class: 'readout' })

function update() {
  const s = source.poll()
  const lines = []
  if (s.leftStickX || s.leftStickY)
    lines.push(`L stick: ${s.leftStickX.toFixed(2)}, ${s.leftStickY.toFixed(2)}`)
  if (s.rightStickX || s.rightStickY)
    lines.push(`R stick: ${s.rightStickX.toFixed(2)}, ${s.rightStickY.toFixed(2)}`)
  const btns = ['buttonA','buttonB','buttonX','buttonY',
    'leftBumper','rightBumper','leftTrigger','rightTrigger',
    'dpadUp','dpadDown','dpadLeft','dpadRight']
    .filter(k => s[k] > 0)
  if (btns.length) lines.push(`Buttons: ${btns.join(', ')}`)
  if (customBtns.size) lines.push(`Custom: ${[...customBtns].join(', ')}`)
  readout.textContent = lines.join('\n') || 'Touch or click the gamepad'
  requestAnimationFrame(update)
}
update()

preview.append(div({ class: 'gamepad-demo' }, pad, readout))
```
```css
.gamepad-demo {
  display: flex;
  flex-direction: column;
  align-items: center;
  gap: 4px;
}
.gamepad-demo svg {
  width: 100%;
  max-width: 400px;
  cursor: pointer;
}
.gamepad-demo .readout {
  font-family: ui-monospace, monospace;
  font-size: 12px;
  color: #222;
  min-height: 2.5em;
  margin: 0;
}
.gamepad-demo [data-part].active {
  stroke-width: 24;
  filter: brightness(1.3);
}
```

## How it works

Load any SVG with elements whose `data-part` values match the standard layout:

| `data-part` | VirtualGamepad | Type |
|-------------|---------------|------|
| `left_stick` / `left_stick_travel` | `leftStickX/Y` | stick |
| `right_stick` / `right_stick_travel` | `rightStickX/Y` | stick |
| `A`, `B`, `X`, `Y` | `buttonA/B/X/Y` | button |
| `left_bumper`, `right_bumper` | `leftBumper`, `rightBumper` | button |
| `left_trigger`, `right_trigger` | `leftTrigger`, `rightTrigger` | button |
| `dpad_up/down/left/right` | `dpadUp/Down/Left/Right` | button |
| `menu`, `view` | `menu`, `view` | button |
| `left_stick_click`, `right_stick_click` | `leftStickPress`, `rightStickPress` | button |

### Sticks

Touch inside a `*_stick_travel` region to grab the stick. The travel circle
recenters to your touch point and the knob tracks your drag. Release snaps
everything back. Deadzone and max-zone are applied so you don't need pixel
precision.

### Buttons

Touch a button element to set its value to 1 and add an `active` CSS class.
Release sets it back to 0. Elements with `data-part` values not in the table
above still get the `active` class and fire the optional
`handleButton(part, pressed)` callback.

## Usage

```typescript
import { TouchGamepadSource } from 'tosijs-3d'

const svg = document.querySelector('svg.gamepad')
const source = new TouchGamepadSource(svg)

// Add to a MappedInputProvider alongside keyboard/hardware gamepad
provider.addSource(source)
```

# auto-rig

**A character nobody rigged, walking.** Four meshes as they came out of a
modelling program (no skeleton, no weights) are rigged in the browser and play
UAL's clips: eight markers per mesh place a 65-bone skeleton (`fitSkeleton`),
bone heat binds the skin to it (`boneHeat`), `buildRig` makes a Babylon
skeleton of the result, and `retargetClip` plays the clips on it. The weasels
have tails, which no clip animates, so their tails run on spring bones.

## Demo

Omnidude, a weasel, a skinny weasel and a space marine (shown at 42% of his
4.4 m), by Tonio Loewald, [CC BY 4.0](https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/).

Open the panel (the owl) to **rig one yourself**: pick a character and its
front view appears with the eight markers on it. Drag a marker (the other side
follows while symmetry is on) and when you let go the character is fitted,
bound and walking again. **guess** places the markers from the silhouette
alone, which is where you would start with a new mesh. The weasels' tail tips
are placed in the side view. The clip, the bones and tail stiffness are below.

```js
import {
  b3d, b3dLight, assetUrl, select3d, slider3d, toggle3d, button3d, label3d,
  objTriangles, fitSkeleton, meshQuery, skinSegments, boneHeat, buildRig,
  readSkeleton, retargetClip, springNodes, markerField3d,
} from 'tosijs-3d'
import { tosi } from 'tosijs'

const { autorig } = tosi({
  autorig: { who: 'skinny weasel', clip: 'Walk_Loop', bones: false, tail: 0.25, symmetry: true },
})
const clips = ['Idle_Loop', 'Walk_Loop', 'Jog_Fwd_Loop', 'Sprint_Loop', 'Dance_Loop', 'Crouch_Fwd_Loop']

// Mixamo's eight markers on the front view (the character's left is +X),
// given for the left side and mirrored.
const markers = (chin, groin, wrist, elbow, knee) => {
  const p = ([x, y]) => ({ x, y })
  const m = ([x, y]) => ({ x: -x, y })
  return {
    chin: p(chin), groin: p(groin),
    wristL: p(wrist), wristR: m(wrist), elbowL: p(elbow), elbowR: m(elbow), kneeL: p(knee), kneeR: m(knee),
  }
}
const cast = [
  { name: 'omnidude', file: 'omnidude.obj', objects: ['Omnidude', 'Head'], color: [0.55, 0.6, 0.7],
    markers: markers([0, 1.338], [0, 0.467], [0.74, 1.075], [0.47, 1.095], [0.152, 0.258]) },
  { name: 'weasel', file: 'weasel.obj', objects: ['Weasel'], color: [0.6, 0.42, 0.25], tail: { x: 0, y: 0.5, z: -0.95 },
    markers: markers([0, 0.967], [0, 0.358], [0.51, 0.763], [0.397, 0.763], [0.142, 0.174]) },
  { name: 'skinny weasel', file: 'weasel-skinny.obj', objects: ['Body', 'Head', 'Tail'], color: [0.8, 0.5, 0.25], tail: { x: 0, y: 0.77, z: -0.95 },
    markers: markers([0, 1.259], [0, 0.64], [0.657, 1.154], [0.404, 1.14], [0.106, 0.34]) },
  { name: 'space marine', file: 'space-marine.glb', mesh: 'EvilMarineBody', color: [0.3, 0.4, 0.3], scale: 0.42,
    markers: markers([0, 3.594], [0, 1.656], [1.966, 2.937], [1.18, 3.064], [0.388, 0.943]) },
]

let bodies = []
let field = null

// Tall enough for the marker panel beside the characters.
preview.closest('tosi-example')?.style.setProperty('--tosi-example-height', '560px')
preview.append(
  b3d(
    {
      style: 'width:100%;height:100%',
      clearColor: '#2a3340',
      panelTitle: 'Auto-rig',
      scenePanel: () => {
        const i = cast.findIndex((c) => c.name === autorig.who.value)
        const body = bodies[i]
        field = body
          ? markerField3d({
              positions: body.positions, indices: body.indices,
              value: body.markers, tail: body.tail, symmetry: autorig.symmetry.value,
              handleCommit: (m, t) => { body.markers = m; body.tail = t; body.rig() },
            })
          : null
        return [
          select3d({ label: 'rig', value: autorig.who, options: cast.map((c) => c.name) }),
          field ?? label3d({ text: 'loading…' }),
          toggle3d({ label: 'symmetry', value: autorig.symmetry }),
          button3d({ label: 'guess', handleClick: () => field?.guess() }),
          select3d({ label: 'clip', value: autorig.clip, options: clips }),
          toggle3d({ label: 'bones', value: autorig.bones }),
          slider3d({ label: 'tail stiffness', value: autorig.tail, min: 0, max: 1, step: 0.01 }),
        ]
      },
      async sceneCreated(el, BABYLON) {
        const scene = el.scene
        const cam = new BABYLON.ArcRotateCamera('c', Math.PI / 2, 1.35, 6.5, new BABYLON.Vector3(0, 0.8, 0), scene)
        cam.attachControl(el.querySelector('canvas'), true)
        el.setActiveCamera(cam)
        const ground = BABYLON.MeshBuilder.CreateGround('g', { width: 12, height: 6 }, scene)
        ground.position.y = -0.01

        // The meshes, as triangles in the file's own (right-handed) frame.
        const triangles = async (c) => {
          const url = assetUrl('tonio/rigging/' + c.file)
          if (c.file.endsWith('.obj')) return objTriangles(await (await fetch(url)).text(), c.objects)
          const glb = await BABYLON.LoadAssetContainerAsync(url, scene)
          const m = glb.meshes.find((m) => m.name === c.mesh)
          const out = { positions: Array.from(m.getVerticesData('position')), indices: Array.from(m.getIndices()) }
          glb.dispose()
          return out
        }

        // UAL's mannequin is only the clips' source: it stays hidden.
        const ual = await BABYLON.LoadAssetContainerAsync(assetUrl('quaternius/UAL1_core.glb', 2), scene)
        ual.addAllToScene()
        for (const g of ual.animationGroups) g.stop()
        const ualRoot = ual.meshes.find((m) => m.name === '__root__')
        const source = readSkeleton(ual.skeletons[0], ualRoot)
        ualRoot.setEnabled(false)

        // Retarget a clip to a body the first time it is asked for.
        const clipFor = (body, name) => {
          if (!body.clips.has(name)) {
            const clip = ual.animationGroups.find((g) => g.name === name)
            body.clips.set(name, clip ? retargetClip(clip, source, body.read, { scene }) : null)
          }
          return body.clips.get(name)
        }
        const play = (body) => {
          body.playing?.stop()
          body.playing = clipFor(body, autorig.clip.value)
          body.playing?.start(true)
        }

        bodies = []
        for (const [i, c] of cast.entries()) {
          const { positions, indices } = await triangles(c)
          const mat = new BABYLON.StandardMaterial(c.file, scene)
          mat.diffuseColor = new BABYLON.Color3(...c.color)
          // Place and scale by a node above the rig's own (mirrored) root.
          const stand = new BABYLON.TransformNode(c.file + '-stand', scene)
          stand.position.x = 2.4 - 1.6 * i
          stand.scaling.setAll(c.scale ?? 1)
          const body = { positions, indices, markers: c.markers, tail: c.tail ?? null, clips: new Map() }
          // Fit, bind and build, then play: again whenever the markers move.
          body.rig = () => {
            for (const g of body.clips.values()) g?.dispose()
            body.clips.clear()
            body.playing = null
            body.spring?.dispose()
            body.viewer?.dispose()
            body.built?.dispose()
            const rig = fitSkeleton(body.markers, meshQuery(positions, indices), { tail: body.tail ?? undefined })
            const weights = boneHeat(positions, indices, skinSegments(rig))
            const built = buildRig(scene, c.file, positions, indices, rig, weights)
            built.mesh.material = mat
            built.root.parent = stand
            const tail = ['tail_01', 'tail_02', 'tail_03', 'tail_04', 'tail_05', 'tail_leaf'].map((n) => built.node(n))
            body.spring = tail[0] ? springNodes(scene, tail, { params: { stiffness: autorig.tail.value } }) : null
            body.viewer = new BABYLON.SkeletonViewer(built.skeleton, built.mesh, scene, false, 3, { displayMode: 0 })
            body.viewer.isEnabled = autorig.bones.value
            body.built = built
            body.read = readSkeleton(built.skeleton, built.root)
            play(body)
          }
          body.rig()
          bodies.push(body)
        }
        el.refreshScenePanel()

        autorig.who.observe(() => el.refreshScenePanel())
        autorig.symmetry.observe(() => { if (field) field.symmetry = autorig.symmetry.value })
        autorig.clip.observe(() => { for (const b of bodies) play(b) })
        autorig.bones.observe(() => { for (const b of bodies) b.viewer.isEnabled = autorig.bones.value })
        autorig.tail.observe(() => { for (const b of bodies) if (b.spring) b.spring.params.stiffness = autorig.tail.value })
      },
    },
    b3dLight({ intensity: 0.9 })
  )
)
```

## How it is built

AUTO-RIG, THE BABYLON SIDE: a fitted skeleton (`rig-fit`) and its skin
weights (`bone-heat`) become a real Babylon skeleton on a mesh, built the way
the glTF loader builds one, so everything that works on a loaded character
works on it: `readSkeleton` and `retargetClip` play UAL clips on it, and
`springNodes` simulates its tail.

The joints are TransformNodes under one container (`root`), each bone LINKED
to its node, with the mesh under the same container: Babylon then reads each
bone from its node, and the skin and the nodes share a frame.

THE DATA IS RIGHT-HANDED, as OBJ and glTF files are (and as `rig-fit`
assumes: a character facing +Z has its left on +X). Babylon is left-handed,
so the container carries the same mirror glTF's `__root__` does (a half turn
about Y and z scaled by -1). Pass `root` to `readSkeleton` as you would
`__root__`: both measure in the file's own frame. Move the character by a
parent of `root`, not `root` itself.

# spring bones

**Hair, tails and cloaks that follow the body.** A chain of bones nobody
animates — a ponytail, a tail, a strand of a cape — swings, trails and
settles on its own, after the body's animation has posed it each frame. The
body is animated; the hair is simulated.

## Demo

A UAL mannequin with a ponytail it was not made with: six bones hung from
the head, a tube skinned to them by bone heat, and the chain simulated.
Change the clip, or the stiffness and damping, in the panel.

```js
import { b3d, b3dLight, assetUrl, select3d, slider3d, springBones, boneHeat, sceneDelta } from 'tosijs-3d'
import { tosi } from 'tosijs'

const { hair } = tosi({ hair: { clip: 'Jog_Fwd_Loop', stiffness: 0.1, damping: 0.6 } })
const clips = ['Idle_Loop', 'Walk_Loop', 'Jog_Fwd_Loop', 'Sprint_Loop', 'Dance_Loop']
const speeds = { Idle_Loop: 0, Walk_Loop: 1.4, Jog_Fwd_Loop: 3, Sprint_Loop: 5.5, Dance_Loop: 0 }

preview.append(
  b3d(
    {
      clearColor: '#2a3340',
      scenePanelOpen: true,
      panelTitle: 'Ponytail',
      scenePanel: () => [
        select3d({ label: 'clip', value: hair.clip, options: clips }),
        slider3d({ label: 'stiffness', value: hair.stiffness, min: 0, max: 1, step: 0.01 }),
        slider3d({ label: 'damping', value: hair.damping, min: 0, max: 0.99, step: 0.01 }),
      ],
      async sceneCreated(el, BABYLON) {
        const scene = el.scene
        const cam = new BABYLON.ArcRotateCamera('c', Math.PI / 3, 1.3, 3.2, new BABYLON.Vector3(0, 1, 0), scene)
        cam.attachControl(el.querySelector('canvas'), true)
        el.setActiveCamera(cam)
        BABYLON.MeshBuilder.CreateGround('g', { width: 12, height: 12 }, scene)

        const ual = await BABYLON.LoadAssetContainerAsync(assetUrl('quaternius/UAL1_core.glb', 2), scene)
        ual.addAllToScene()
        for (const g of ual.animationGroups) g.stop()
        const root = ual.meshes.find((m) => m.name === '__root__')
        const node = (n) => ual.transformNodes.find((t) => t.name === n)
        const head = node('Head')

        // Where the ponytail hangs, measured off the body in its rest pose:
        // forward is from the heel to the toes, up the back of the skull.
        root.computeWorldMatrix(true)
        for (const t of ual.transformNodes) t.computeWorldMatrix(true)
        const at = (n) => node(n).getAbsolutePosition().clone()
        const fwd = at('ball_leaf_l').subtract(at('foot_l')).multiplyByFloats(1, 0, 1).normalize()
        const up = new BABYLON.Vector3(0, 1, 0)
        const start = at('Head').add(up.scale(0.13)).subtract(fwd.scale(0.1))
        const dir = fwd.scale(-0.45).add(up.scale(-0.9)).normalize()
        const points = [0, 1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6].map((i) => start.add(dir.scale(0.075 * i)))

        const pony = springBones(scene, head, points, {
          colliders: [
            { node: head, radius: 0.12, offset: up.scale(0.09) },
            { node: node('spine_03'), radius: 0.17 },
            { node: node('clavicle_l'), radius: 0.09 },
            { node: node('clavicle_r'), radius: 0.09 },
          ],
          params: { stiffness: hair.stiffness.value, damping: hair.damping.value },
        })
        hair.stiffness.observe(() => (pony.params.stiffness = hair.stiffness.value))
        hair.damping.observe(() => (pony.params.damping = hair.damping.value))

        // A tube around the chain, skinned to it by bone heat.
        const tube = pony.skinTube({ radius: 0.035, tipRadius: 0.012, sides: 8, boneHeat })
        const mat = new BABYLON.StandardMaterial('hair', scene)
        mat.diffuseColor = new BABYLON.Color3(0.45, 0.25, 0.1)
        mat.backFaceCulling = false
        tube.material = mat

        // Run round a circle at the clip's own speed, so the hair trails.
        // (A node above glTF's root, whose mirror and turn stay as loaded.)
        const runner = new BABYLON.TransformNode('runner', scene)
        root.parent = runner
        const yaw0 = Math.atan2(fwd.x, fwd.z)
        // The camera follows the runner, at head height.
        const look = new BABYLON.TransformNode('look', scene)
        look.parent = runner
        look.position.y = 1.35
        cam.setTarget(look)
        let angle = 0
        const radius = 2.2
        scene.onBeforeAnimationsObservable.add(() => {
          angle += ((speeds[hair.clip.value] ?? 0) * sceneDelta(scene)) / radius
          runner.position.set(Math.cos(angle) * radius, 0, Math.sin(angle) * radius)
          runner.rotation.y = Math.atan2(-Math.sin(angle), Math.cos(angle)) - yaw0
        })
        let playing = null
        const play = (name) => {
          playing?.stop()
          playing = ual.animationGroups.find((g) => g.name === name)
          playing?.start(true)
        }
        play(hair.clip.value)
        hair.clip.observe(() => play(hair.clip.value))
      },
    },
    b3dLight({ intensity: 0.9 })
  )
)
```

## Use

`springBones(scene, anchor, points, options)` hangs a chain from `anchor` (any
TransformNode: a glTF bone's node, or a mesh) through `points` (world, in the
rest pose), makes a TransformNode per joint under the anchor, and simulates
them every frame after animations run. Options:

| option | |
| --- | --- |
| `params` | `stiffness` (0 hangs free, 1 holds its rest shape), `damping` (velocity lost per second), `gravity`, `radius` |
| `colliders` | spheres the chain stays out of: `{ node, radius, offset? }` |

It returns `{ nodes, params, skinTube(options), reset(), dispose() }`.
`nodes` are the chain's joints, to parent your own meshes to; `skinTube`
builds a tube skinned to them (pass `boneHeat` to weight it). Call `reset()`
after a teleport. The pure model is `spring-chain`: Verlet at a fixed 120 Hz
step, lengths solved from the root with Müller's follow-the-leader damping
(otherwise the tip cracks like a whip for seconds).

# character builder

**Make a person, and watch them walk.** The first steps of the character
creator (CHARACTER-DESIGN.md): MakeHuman's CC0 body
([human-pack](/human-pack/)), reshaped by sliders for gender, age, muscle,
weight, height and ancestry ([human-morph](/human-morph/)), with UAL's
skeleton placed from its own joint cubes ([human-rig](/human-rig/)) so it
plays UAL's clips at any shape.

## Demo

Open the settings (the owl): the body sliders reshape it
while it walks, since every body carries its own joints. The skin weights are
bone heat's, worked out from the mesh once when the page loads. The eyes
([human-face](/human-face/)) follow the camera, and the hair
([human-hair](/human-hair/)) is Quaternius' CC0 hairstyles, made for another
head and fitted to this one.

```js
import { b3d, b3dSun, b3dLight, slider3d, select3d, toggle3d, label3d, assetUrl, loadHumanPack, macroWeights, morphHuman, DEFAULT_HUMAN_BODY, humanRig, humanSkin, skinMaterial, lipTint, humanFace, eyeGaze, eyeTexture, eyeMaterial, lashMaterial, loadHairLibrary, headScalp, hairMaterial, humanHair, skinSegments, boneHeat, buildRig, readSkeleton, retargetClip } from 'tosijs-3d'
import { tosi, elements } from 'tosijs'
const { div } = elements

const status = div({ class: 'status' }, 'loading the animations…')
const { walker } = tosi({ walker: { ...DEFAULT_HUMAN_BODY, gender: 1, clip: 'Walk_Loop', tone: 0.3, redness: 0.5, iris: 0.2, hair: 'Hair_SimpleParted', beard: 'none', brows: 'Eyebrows_Regular', hairLight: 0.25, hairRed: 0.15, paused: false } })
let mat = null
let paint = () => {}
let eyes = null
let dress = () => {}
// Filled when the hair library loads.
const styles = { hair: ['none'], beard: ['none'], brows: ['none'] }
const clips = ['Idle_Loop', 'Walk_Loop', 'Jog_Fwd_Loop', 'Sprint_Loop', 'Dance_Loop', 'Crouch_Fwd_Loop']
let rebuild = () => {}

const slider = (label, key) =>
  slider3d({ label, value: walker[key], min: 0, max: 1, step: 0.01 })

preview.append(
  b3d(
    {
      clearColor: '#2a3340',
      panelTitle: 'Walker',
      scenePanel: () => [
        select3d({ label: 'clip', value: walker.clip, options: clips }),
        toggle3d({ label: 'pause', value: walker.paused }),
        slider('gender', 'gender'),
        slider('age', 'age'),
        slider('muscle', 'muscle'),
        slider('weight', 'weight'),
        slider('height', 'height'),
        label3d({ text: 'Skin' }),
        slider3d({ label: 'tone', value: walker.tone, min: 0, max: 1, step: 0.01 }),
        slider3d({ label: 'redness', value: walker.redness, min: 0, max: 1, step: 0.01 }),
        slider3d({ label: 'eyes', value: walker.iris, min: 0, max: 1, step: 0.01 }),
        label3d({ text: 'Hair' }),
        select3d({ label: 'hair', value: walker.hair, options: styles.hair }),
        select3d({ label: 'beard', value: walker.beard, options: styles.beard }),
        select3d({ label: 'brows', value: walker.brows, options: styles.brows }),
        slider3d({ label: 'light', value: walker.hairLight, min: 0, max: 1, step: 0.01 }),
        slider3d({ label: 'red', value: walker.hairRed, min: 0, max: 1, step: 0.01 }),
        label3d({ text: 'Ancestry' }),
        slider('african', 'african'),
        slider('asian', 'asian'),
        slider('caucasian', 'caucasian'),
      ],
      async sceneCreated(el, BABYLON) {
        const scene = el.scene
        const cam = new BABYLON.ArcRotateCamera('c', Math.PI / 2 - 0.5, 1.35, 4, new BABYLON.Vector3(0, 0.9, 0), scene)
        cam.minZ = 0.05
        cam.attachControl(el.querySelector('canvas'), true)
        el.setActiveCamera(cam)
        BABYLON.MeshBuilder.CreateGround('g', { width: 8, height: 8 }, scene)

        // UAL's mannequin is only the clips' source: it stays hidden.
        const ual = await BABYLON.LoadAssetContainerAsync(assetUrl('quaternius/UAL1_core.glb', 2), scene)
        status.textContent = 'loading the body…'
        ual.addAllToScene()
        for (const g of ual.animationGroups) g.stop()
        const ualRoot = ual.meshes.find((m) => m.name === '__root__')
        const source = readSkeleton(ual.skeletons[0], ualRoot)
        ualRoot.setEnabled(false)

        const pack = await loadHumanPack(assetUrl('makehuman/hm08/hm08.json', 4)).catch((e) => {
          status.textContent = `could not load the body: ${e.message}`
          throw e
        })
        // Quaternius' hairstyles, made for its own head, fitted to each body.
        const hairLib = await loadHairLibrary(scene, assetUrl('quaternius/libraries/hairstyles.glb'))
        const adult = (n) => !n.endsWith('_Teen')
        const facial = (n) => /Beard|Moustache|MuttonChops/.test(n)
        styles.hair.push(...hairLib.names('hair').filter((n) => adult(n) && !facial(n)))
        styles.beard.push(...hairLib.names('hair').filter((n) => adult(n) && facial(n)))
        styles.brows.push(...hairLib.names('eyebrows').filter(adult))
        const tints = new Map()
        const tint = (part) => {
          const from = part.material
          if (!tints.has(from)) tints.set(from, hairMaterial(scene, from, `hair-${tints.size}`))
          return tints.get(from)
        }
        status.textContent = 'binding the skin…'
        // Let the message paint before the binding takes the thread.
        await new Promise((r) => setTimeout(r, 50))
        const skin = humanSkin(pack)
        const [lips, eyePart, lashPart] = await Promise.all([pack.region('lips'), pack.part('eyes'), pack.part('eyelashes')])
        eyes = eyeTexture(scene, { iris: walker.iris.value })
        const faceMaterials = { eye: eyeMaterial(scene, eyes), lashes: lashMaterial(scene) }
        let face = null
        let scalp = null
        let hairMeshes = []
        dress = () => {
          for (const m of tints.values()) m.setHair(walker.hairLight.value, walker.hairRed.value)
          if (!built || !scalp) return
          for (const m of hairMeshes) m.dispose()
          hairMeshes = []
          for (const key of ['hair', 'beard', 'brows']) {
            const parts = hairLib.piece(walker[key].value)
            if (!parts) continue
            // Brows lie on the skin; hair stands a little off it.
            const margin = key === 'brows' ? 0.0015 : 0.003
            const centreY = key === 'brows' ? scalp.brow : undefined
            hairMeshes.push(...humanHair(scene, built, built.rig, scalp, parts, tint, { margin, name: key, centreY }))
          }
          el.register({ meshes: hairMeshes })
        }
        for (const key of ['hair', 'beard', 'brows']) walker[key].observe(() => dress())
        // Colours repaint whoever sets them: a slider, or code.
        const recolour = () => { for (const m of tints.values()) m.setHair(walker.hairLight.value, walker.hairRed.value) }
        walker.hairLight.observe(recolour)
        walker.hairRed.observe(recolour)
        for (const key of Object.keys(DEFAULT_HUMAN_BODY)) walker[key].observe(() => rebuild())
        walker.tone.observe(() => paint())
        walker.redness.observe(() => paint())
        walker.iris.observe(() => eyes.setIris(walker.iris.value))
        // The eyes find the camera.
        eyeGaze(scene, { get eyes() { return face?.eyes ?? [] }, get forward() { return face?.forward ?? [] } }, () => cam.position)
        const body = () => {
          const b = {}
          for (const k of Object.keys(DEFAULT_HUMAN_BODY)) b[k] = Number(walker[k].value)
          return macroWeights(b, (n) => pack.has(n))
        }
        // The weights depend only on the topology: once, on the first body.
        const first = await morphHuman(pack, body())
        // The weights depend only on the topology: once, on the first body.
        const rig0 = humanRig(pack.manifest, first)
        const weights = boneHeat(skin.positions(first), skin.triangles, skinSegments(rig0))

        mat = skinMaterial(scene, { tone: walker.tone.value, redness: walker.redness.value })
        // The skin's colour, and the lips' as vertex colours over it.
        paint = () => {
          mat.setSkin(walker.tone.value, walker.redness.value)
          if (lips && built) {
            built.mesh.setVerticesData('color', lipTint(skin, lips, walker.tone.value, walker.redness.value))
          }
        }
        const stand = new BABYLON.TransformNode('stand', scene)
        let built = null
        let playing = null
        let read = null
        const play = () => {
          playing?.dispose()
          const clip = ual.animationGroups.find((g) => g.name === walker.clip.value)
          playing = clip ? retargetClip(clip, source, read, { scene }) : null
          playing?.start(true)
          if (walker.paused.value) playing?.pause()
        }
        let busy = false
        let again = false
        rebuild = async () => {
          if (busy) { again = true; return }
          busy = true
          const full = await morphHuman(pack, body())
          const rig = humanRig(pack.manifest, full)
          face?.dispose()
          built?.dispose()
          built = buildRig(scene, 'walker', skin.positions(full), skin.triangles, rig, weights)
          face = humanFace(scene, built, rig, pack.manifest, full, { eyes: eyePart, eyelashes: lashPart }, faceMaterials)
          built.rig = rig
          scalp = headScalp(pack.manifest, full, pack.triangles, hairLib.reference)
          dress()
          built.mesh.material = mat
          built.root.parent = stand
          stand.position.y = -rig.at('root').y
          el.register({ meshes: [built.mesh, ...face.meshes] })
          paint()
          read = readSkeleton(built.skeleton, built.root)
          play()
          busy = false
          if (again) { again = false; rebuild() }
        }
        await rebuild()
        status.remove()
        walker.clip.observe(play)
        walker.paused.observe(() => (walker.paused.value ? playing?.pause() : playing?.play(true)))
      },
    },
    b3dSun({ timeOfDay: 15 }),
    b3dLight({ intensity: 0.5 })
  ),
  status
)
